    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The fleets were of different kinds: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a>'s heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> were hard to board but slow, and Octavian's smaller Liburnian vessels could get in close, attack the crews and retreat.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a>'s side was weakened before the fighting began by desertion, disease and a severe malaria outbreak, and by the defection of his general Quintus Dellius with his battle plans.</li><li>Historians generally view the consolidation of power that followed as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a> came to Actium with around 500 ships but could not man all of them, and for the battle itself it is estimated that he had around 140 ships against Octavian's 260.</p></aside>
    </section>

