    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">37 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> is renewed for five years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">34 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra</a> raise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesarion">Caesarion</a> to power with the title King of the Kings</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">32 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a> divorces Octavia and goes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> declares war on Cleopatra</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">31 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a>'s forces are blockaded at Actium while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Agrippa</a> raids the Greek coast</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">31 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a>'s fleet sails out of the straits and is routed by Octavian's fleet</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">30 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a> is beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and dies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra</a> kills herself</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">29 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Octavian founds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicopolis-epirus">Nicopolis</a> to commemorate the victory</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">27 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Senate awards Octavian the title Augustus</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-actium" data-v="95dea998f9" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Antony's fleet comes out and forms line</b> Octavian's fleet waited beyond the straits. Antony led his ships out through the straits and they formed a line, with Cleopatra's squadron behind them, and stayed quiet.</li><li><b>Octavian steers to the right</b> After a short hesitation Octavian ordered his ships to steer to the right and pass the enemy's ships. Fearing that his line would be surrounded, Antony was forced to give the order to attack.</li><li><b>Sosius attacks and Octavian's left stays out of range</b> Sosius opened the attack from Antony's left wing. Antony hoped to drive back Agrippa's wing at the north end of his line with his biggest ships, but Octavian's fleet stayed out of range.</li><li><b>The fleets fight through the afternoon</b> The two lines closed after midday and fought on all afternoon. Antony's larger ships rained missiles on the smaller ones, which closed in, shot at the crews and drew off again, and neither side gained a clear result.</li><li><b>Cleopatra's squadron sails for the open sea</b> Cleopatra's squadron, in the rear, took no part in the fighting and retreated to the open sea. A breeze sprang up in the right direction and the ships were soon out of sight.</li><li><b>Antony follows and the rest are taken</b> Antony thought all was lost and followed the fleeing squadron. He moved to a smaller vessel and slipped away with a few ships as escort, and the ships left behind were captured or sunk.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mark-antony.jpg" alt="Mark Antony" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octavian_and_Antony_denarius_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Byzantium565, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> of Octavian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Lepidus was renewed for five years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a> in 37 BCE. It broke down when Octavian came to see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesarion">Caesarion</a>, the professed son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, as a major threat, and when Antony abandoned his wife Octavia Minor, Octavian's sister, and moved to Egypt. Octavian's standing rested on Caesar's legacy of 44 BCE, which made him Caesar's adopted son and heir, while Antony had been Caesar's most successful senior officer and claimed much of the support of Caesar's soldiers and veterans.</p><p>In 34 BCE Antony and Cleopatra raised Caesarion, then 13, to power with the title King of the Kings. Octavian began a propaganda war, denouncing Antony as an enemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> who intended to set up a monarchy on Caesarion's behalf and move the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. Each man also had specific complaints against the other. The triumvirate expired on the last day of 33 BCE.</p><p>In 32 BCE one third of the Senate and both consuls, Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus and Gaius Sosius, joined Antony. He divorced Octavia and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> with Cleopatra, where a vast fleet gathered, a large proportion of it furnished by her. Octavian obtained Antony's deposition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> of 31 BCE and a proclamation of war against Cleopatra, which was understood to mean Antony.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Actium&#8221; cites where it covers Background:</p><ul><li>Evelyn Shirley Shuckburgh, A History of Rome to the Battle of Actium (Macmillan and Company, 1917)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa.jpg" alt="Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrippa_Gabii_Louvre_Ma1208.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Figures differ. In early 31 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a> is said to have had 500 ships and 70,000 infantry and Octavian 400 ships and 80,000 infantry. On the morning of 2 September Antony is credited with 250 larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> with towers full of armed men and Octavian 400 galleys, and another estimate gives Antony around 140 ships against 260 for Octavian. Antony had arrived with around 500 ships but could not man them all. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and Dio speak of desertion and disease in his camp, and many ships were short of rowers after a severe malaria outbreak.</p><p>Antony's ships were mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quinquereme">quinqueremes</a> with smaller quadriremes, heavier and wider than Octavian's. His flagship was a ten, and an eight carried around 200 heavy marines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and at least six ballistae. They were very difficult to board but less manoeuvrable. Octavian's fleet was largely of smaller Liburnian vessels, with better trained, well-fed and rested crews.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Agrippa</a> commanded Octavian's left wing, Lucius Arruntius the centre and Marcus Lurius the right, while Titus Statilius Taurus commanded the army on the shore north of the straits. Antony and Lucius Gellius Poplicola held Antony's right wing, Marcus Octavius and Marcus Insteius the centre and Gaius Sosius the left, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra</a>'s squadron behind. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-canidius-crassus">Publius Canidius Crassus</a> commanded the land forces. Before the battle Quintus Dellius defected to Octavian with Antony's battle plans.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Actium&#8221; cites where it covers Order of battle:</p><ul><li>Evelyn Shirley Shuckburgh, A History of Rome to the Battle of Actium (Macmillan and Company, 1917)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii.jpg" alt="Cleopatra VII" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kleopatra-VII.-Altes-Museum-Berlin1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Louis le Grand, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a> had first planned a descent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at the end of 32 BCE but found the sea guarded and wintered at Patrae, with his fleet in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambracian-gulf">Ambracian Gulf</a> and his army near the promontory of Actium. In 31 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Agrippa</a> raided Greek towns along the coast and Octavian's cavalry won skirmishes, so that Antony confined his soldiers to the southern camp. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra</a> advised garrisoning strong towns and returning the main fleet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and this was apparently agreed. Octavian first considered letting Antony sail and then attacking him, but Agrippa persuaded him to give battle.</p><p>On 1 September Octavian addressed his fleet. The next day was wet and the sea rough. Antony's fleet came out of the straits and formed a line and remained quiet. Octavian ordered his ships to steer right and pass the enemy, and Antony, fearing to be surrounded, gave the word to attack. Antony had hoped to drive back Agrippa's wing with his biggest ships, but Octavian's fleet, aware of this, stayed out of range. The battle raged all afternoon without a decisive result.</p><p>Cleopatra's fleet in the rear then retreated to the open sea without engaging. Antony, who had not observed the signal, believed all was lost and followed. Many ships raised sail and threw their towers overboard. Some fought on until long after nightfall, when many were blazing from firebrands. Antony burned the ships he could no longer man, transferred to a smaller vessel and escaped with a few ships. Those left behind were captured or sunk.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Actium&#8221; cites where it covers Battle, Order of battle, Combat:</p><ul><li>Evelyn Shirley Shuckburgh, A History of Rome to the Battle of Actium (Macmillan and Company, 1917)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/caesarion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caesarion.jpg" alt="Caesarion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/caesarion">Caesarion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caesarion_granite_head.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">carolemage (Flickr), CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Octavian spent the night on board saving the crews of the burning vessels. The next day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a>'s camp was occupied. Under cover of darkness some 19 legions and 12,000 cavalry had fled before Antony could fight a land battle, and the army that had been equal to Octavian's deserted. Octavian visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and wintered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, though he had to go briefly to Brundisium to settle a mutiny and arrange land assignments.</p><p>At Samos he received a message from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra</a> with a gold crown and throne, offering to abdicate in favour of her sons. In spring Cornelius Gallus advanced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraetonium">Paraetonium</a> and Octavian landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a>. Antony was defeated by Gallus and, after a minor victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on 31 July 30 BCE, was decisively beaten in a general attack. Believing false rumours that Cleopatra had killed herself, he stabbed himself in the stomach, and died in her arms in the mausoleum where she was hiding.</p><p>Cleopatra killed herself on 12 August 30 BCE. Octavian had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesarion">Caesarion</a> killed later that month, and also Antony's elder son Antyllus, but spared the children of Antony and Cleopatra. He gave Antony and Cleopatra a public military funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> became a Roman province, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> and the Hellenistic Period.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Actium&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Evelyn Shirley Shuckburgh, A History of Rome to the Battle of Actium (Macmillan and Company, 1917)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and Dio record the desertion and disease that plagued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a>'s camp. In the traditional narrative of the battle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra</a>'s squadron retreats and Antony follows it. Lange argues that Antony would have had victory within reach were it not for Cleopatra's retreat.</p><p>J. M. Carter gives a differing account. In it Antony knew he was surrounded and gathered his ships in a quasi-horseshoe close to the shore. He then sent the northern ships out to attack, had Sosius spread the southern ones, and with Cleopatra sped through the gap left in Octavian's formation, abandoning his entire force. That reading makes the escape Antony's plan and not a flight.</p><p>The figures for the fleets disagree: 500 ships for Antony and 400 for Octavian early in the year, 250 and 400 on the morning of the battle, and an estimate of 140 against 260. Octavian's victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> is dated either 31 July or 1 August 30 BCE. Pelling notes that two former consuls commanded Antony's wings, which suggests that the major action was expected there. A suggestion by scientists researching the dead water phenomenon is that the Egyptian fleet may have been trapped in dead water, which can substantially reduce a ship's speed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Octavian's victory gave him sole, uncontested control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mare-nostrum">Mare Nostrum</a>, the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and consolidated his power over every Roman institution. He adopted the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">Princeps</a>, first citizen, and in 27 BCE the Senate awarded him the title Augustus. He kept the trappings of a restored Republican leader, but historians generally view his consolidation of power and his honorifics as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>To commemorate the victory Octavian founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicopolis-epirus">Nicopolis</a>, City of Victory, in 29 BCE on the southernmost promontory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, opposite Actium. On a hill just north of it, where he had made his camp, he built the Campsite Memorial, a tropaion dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neptune">Neptune</a>, Mars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, which displayed bronze rams taken from captured warships. At the cape of Actium he built the Actian dekanaia, a tropaion made of ten captured warships. The Temple of Apollo Aktios, a deity Octavian repeatedly invoked after the war, was renovated and enlarged.</p><p>Octavian's triple triumph on his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, spread over three days, celebrated his victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>, the Battle of Actium and his conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He was given credit for pardoning many of his opponents after the battle, although he had previously shown little mercy to surrendered enemies.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Actium on 2 September 31 BCE the fleet of Octavian under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Agrippa</a> routed the fleets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra</a>, who escaped with a few ships. Antony's army deserted, both leaders died the next year, and Octavian emerged as sole ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</em></p>

